<article class="env-lemma" id="0B62">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0B62">Lemma <span data-tag="0B62">53.6.4</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/0B5D" data-tag="0B5D">53.6.2</a>. Let $\mathcal{L}$ be an invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module which is globally generated and not isomorphic to $\mathcal{O}_ X$. Then $H^1(X, \omega _ X \otimes \mathcal{L}) = 0$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      By duality as discussed in Section <a href="/tag/0B5B" data-tag="0B5B">53.5</a> we have to show that $H^0(X, \mathcal{L}^{\otimes - 1}) = 0$. If not, then we can choose a global section $t$ of $\mathcal{L}^{\otimes - 1}$ and a global section $s$ of $\mathcal{L}$ such that $st \not= 0$. However, then $st$ is a constant multiple of $1$, by our assumption that $H^0(X, \mathcal{O}_ X) = k$. It follows that $\mathcal{L} \cong \mathcal{O}_ X$, which is a contradiction. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>